  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Trump sent revised text to Iranian counterparts on May 31 — CBS and Axios report the edits targeted uranium retrieval mechanics and Hormuz-specific language — rather than signing the agreement his envoys had negotiated. Fars published a same-day rebuttal stating Trump's post "raised issues that contradict the provisions of the agreement's text"; Iranian MFA spokesperson Baghaei characterized US demands as "not aligned with ongoing negotiations." An Iranian diplomatic response is projected around June 3, leaving June 4–5 as the only available window for principal confirmation before the <span class="cite">F3</span> horizon. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>IRGC Operational HQ issued a formal declaration on May 30 designating the Strait of Hormuz as a "vast operational area" from Jask to Siri Island, requiring IRGC Navy permission for all vessel transits and mandatory adherence to designated routes. Separately, Mojtaba Khamenei's written statement coupled nuclear and missile technology as "non-negotiable national assets" and announced "new legal frameworks" for Hormuz management. With Kpler and USNI data at approximately 6 commercial transits per day through April, and ADNOC's CEO projecting full flows no earlier than Q1–Q2 2027, the sequential chain from MOU to mine removal to insurance recovery to carrier return remains unstarted. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span>, <span class="cite">F6</span></p>

      <p>Times of Israel, Haaretz, and JNS confirmed June 1 as the scheduled date for House Committee debate and the first plenary reading of the Knesset dissolution bill; the preliminary reading passed 110-0 on May 20. The first-reading result is unconfirmed as of report time. Netanyahu revived the haredi draft-exemption bill approximately May 28–31 as a procedural delay lever; haredi parties rejected this offer and confirmed a September election preference against Netanyahu's stated October preference. The House Committee has not formally scheduled an accelerated second-reading timetable. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span></p>

      <p>Lebanon ceasefire fourth-round political talks are confirmed for June 2–3 in Washington. Lebanese President Aoun has rejected normalization terms, and the Lebanese health ministry counts 657 killed since the truce began. Hezbollah is not a party to the US-mediated talks and continues to reject disarmament as a precondition. Pentagon military-track talks ran May 29; Axios reported June 1 that US efforts for a durable ceasefire are stalling as Israel examines options including Beirut strikes. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:19%;">Escalation 19%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:63%;">Protracted 63%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:18%;">De-esc 18%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (19%):</strong> Deal failure on both F3 and F4 combined with the IRGC's confirmed 'vast operational area' doctrine and ongoing Lebanon ceasefire violations raises the probability of kinetic escalation — most plausibly a return to direct US–Iran naval confrontation in Hormuz or an Israeli military move toward Beirut.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (63%):</strong> The dominant path: the edit-response negotiating loop runs past June 7 without signature, Hormuz transits remain in single digits, the Knesset dissolution bill advances slowly toward a late-June or July second reading, and no party crosses the threshold that forces a decisive break in either direction.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (18%):</strong> De-escalation requires an MOU by June 7 (F4) that unlocks the sequential chain toward Hormuz normalization — a path that exists but depends on Iran accepting Trump's May 31 uranium retrieval edits within a 3-day diplomatic response window.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRNA and Tasnim foregrounded Pezeshkian's statement that Iran "does not seek nuclear weapons" — framing restraint as a voluntary declaration of intent rather than a US-mandated condition. Domestically, "we chose not to build one" is rhetorically compatible with maintaining enrichment infrastructure; "we accepted a ban" is not. This separation of capability from intent preserves domestic legitimacy for any eventual deal that stops short of uranium destruction. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move</p>

      <p>Fars published two distinct rebukes of Trump's May 31 Truth Social post before Iran's formal diplomatic response: Trump's assertions "contradict the provisions of the agreement's text," specifically on uranium destruction characterization; and Hormuz "will not return to pre-war status" under any deal, with Iranian management authority preserved. Publication before the official diplomatic channel establishes a domestic record that leadership did not accept the revised terms — this advance framing narrows the political space available to any Iranian negotiator considering acceptance of Trump's May 31 enrichment edits. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>Tasnim carried Khamenei's written statement coupling nuclear and missile technology as "non-negotiable national assets" alongside the announcement of "new legal frameworks" for Hormuz. The legal-frameworks framing positions Hormuz management as a question of maritime law rather than military coercion — a distinction directed at non-US international audiences (Gulf GCC, India, China). Khamenei's physical condition and decision-making capacity remain opaque; no live appearance has been attributed to him since the February strikes. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Parliamentary spokesperson Rezaee's characterization of the US text as "more of an American wish-list than a reality" (Tasnim and IRNA, May 31) was not an individual opinion but a coordinated state-media message — the same framing ran across IRNA, Fars, and Tasnim within the same news cycle. The combined May 30–31 output across all three state outlets amounts to a pre-negotiation red-line statement delivered through domestic media channels ahead of the expected June 3 diplomatic response. → supports <span class="cite">F3</span> without move; supports <span class="cite">F4</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

